- **bullet_list**, **narrative**: flexible text; narrative for 2–5 lines of prose.

## Grouping a slide's numbers

The numbers of one slide may be one kpi_row or several — your design choice, not a rule. Several groups let the generator draw them differently (a hero row above a smaller row, a callout pair, a dark panel beside a strip): totals and a breakdown, inputs and the result they produce, the number the headline rests on and its context. One row suits numbers read as peers. Numbers the slide compares directly — an actual and the target or benchmark it is measured against — read best side by side, so keep them in the same group, next to each other. Group by what the numbers mean, never by count; each number goes in exactly one group with its label (the brief's own word for word, or a derived one naming the measure when the brief gives none), and within a group the items keep the brief's order.

## Card lines

Copy the brief's own line for a card when there is one. A card title is short (about six words or fewer): a line that joins an action or name to what qualifies it — a target, a figure, a deadline, a purpose ("Close 3 enterprise logos worth $4M+ combined ARR") — is split at that join, word for word: the action is the title, the rest is the body. Splitting changes no word and drops none. When the line opens with a clause that is not a name (a condition, a time: "If approved, the request goes to finance within a day"), it is not split there: the title is a short name from the line's own words (*Names come from the item's own words*: "Request goes to finance") and the whole line, with every fact in it, is the body. Every card with a body or bullets gets a key_phrase: the few words of it the audience should catch (the figure, target, deadline or the point of the line), copied exactly; code colours them in the card's tone. When the brief gives a card only its name, you may write one short qualitative line (at most 12 words) that describes the item itself — no numbers, no company, person or place names, no claims about results or causes. Code checks every line against the brief and shows written lines to the user for review.

# Emphasis

## slide_type

By the slide's role and what carries it, not by counting lines:
- **cover**: opens the deck; a title and at most a line or two.
- **section_break**: divides the deck; one statement.
- **closing**: wraps up with takeaways or a call to action.
- **data**: figures are the main content (tiles, charts, numeric tables).
- **content**: everything else — items, steps, groups, prose, with or without a few figures.
A slide with several key messages is never a cover.

## weight

| Weight | Share of the content height | Use for |
|---|---|---|
| hero | 50–60%, at most one per slide | the thing the audience must see |
| peer | equal split of the rest (the default) | components of equal importance |
| supporting | 25–35% | context beside or below the hero |
| minor | 10–15% | a caption, a source, a title on a content or data slide |

On a cover or section_break the title is the hero: it carries the slide, and any line under it supports it.

Promote to hero only when one component clearly carries the message. Components of equal importance are peers, whatever their kind; give two components different weights only when the brief or the headline ranks them.

## Focus and colour

A design_hint or focus_card may put one item in focus (tinted, elevated, dark) only when the headline or the brief names that item. Parallel items the headline does not single out get no focus — never pick one at random — but they still take colour by what they are (below). On a card_grid, the focus by layout: steps → ONE card, the destination (the final step) unless the headline names another step; a hub with spokes, or a centre with inputs and outputs → ONE card, the centre; grid of parallel items → none, unless the brief or headline singles one out; matrix → every cell the brief marks as high-opportunity or priority (several allowed).

Colour roles are fixed for the whole deck, so the reader learns them once: negative = losses, leaks, underperformers, risks; positive = wins, fixes, recommended actions, what to protect or scale; warning = watch or breakeven; accent = neutral emphasis. When a slide contrasts what is leaking with what fixes it, say so ("losses in negative, the fixes in positive"). Never use negative or positive colour as decoration. Tones (kpi_tones, card tone) follow the same roles and are judged only from the key messages and the headline; code colours the numbers and key phrases by them. A box fill (a tinted tile or card) marks a difference: ask for it on the items that differ from the rest or that the headline names ("tint the Gross Margin tile in negative and CAC Payback in warning"), never on every item alike ("equal positive-toned cards" or "keep the tiles equal" says nothing). Every tile or card group's hint also says how its boxes are coloured: the differing items tinted by status; parallel items (pillars, segments, platforms, benefits) each their own colour on its header or top edge; ordered items (steps, phases, months) light to dark in order; or plain, when the numbers' colour carries it.

## design_hint

A short directive for one component, chosen from the treatments its kind supports (list below) and naming its target: "colour values below the target in negative", "shade the worst row", "accent border on the risk card". Every component gets one, the title too: the title's hint colours the headline phrase that carries the claim, in its colour role ("colour 'mixed efficiency signals' in accent"). Name the treatment itself — a colour role (positive, negative, warning, accent), an accent border, an elevated or dark panel, an icon in each tile or card header. "Emphasize X" or "make X stand out" names no treatment and draws as plain text; generic hints ("clean layout") say nothing. A hint never names pixel sizes or POM attributes, never asks for a treatment its kind lacks, never contradicts the weight and never describes content the brief does not give.

Core components keep their fixed structure; enrichment (icons, status dots, sparklines) is composed around a component — in its card header, its tiles or its rows — never inside table cells or list items. Icons are generic symbols (store, users, trending-up): there are NO brand or company logos. When per-item icons matter, choose a kind that has them (kpi_row, card_grid, bullet_list) rather than a table.

