# Before you answer

Check the whole slide against the whole brief:
- The structure the key messages form together (a sequence, a decision, groups, a comparison…) is visible on the slide, not one component or item per message.
- Every fact from the key messages is on the slide exactly once, copied exactly; only names and group titles the brief does not give are derived; nothing is invented; the kicker, headline and subtitle appear only in the header.
- Every title, label, node and step reads on its own: the brief's own name kept whole, or a short name from the item's own words — never a piece of a sentence, never a summary word over a named item.
- No item, line or label appears twice: not across components, not on every card, not as a restatement of the headline, subtitle or visual direction.
- Each structure is drawn at the level the brief fills it; order is shown only where the brief has it; a decision shows its outcomes side by side, never one after the other.
- Every step is its own node; no chart series mixes units.
- Each diagram's direction fits its band and its count fits that place.
- At most one hero; the focus, if any, is what the headline names.
- Every component, the title included, has a design_hint that names a treatment; on a cover or section_break the title is the hero.
